# Pronouns a

*wataxi* - I (feminine) <br>
*bocu* - I (masculine) <br>
*anata* - you (formal) <br>
*kimi* - you (intimate) <br>
*omae* - you (casual) <br>

*tachi* is added after the noun/pronoun to make it a plural, 
e.g. *wataxi tachi* means 'we (fem)'

Ringwo has no 3rd-person plural. 
To express it, one uses the word for man, woman, person or the person's name.

*aquer otoco tenu farmu* <br>
He has a farm (lit. the/that man has a farm)

In ringwo, subject and object pronouns are commonly dropped. The 
last sentence can be expressed as:

*tenu farmu* <br>
(He) has a farm

Another example: <br>
*(wataxi/bocu) iru* <br>
(I'm) angry.

*(aquer ren) turanxu (aquer cosa)* <br>
(He/she) slices(it).

# Nouns

Cases: a 

*no* - genitive <br>

*bocu no casa* <br>
my house aa

*de* - instrumental <br>

*(aquer otoco) turanshu de hocho pomodoro.*<br>
He is slicing tomatoes with a knife.

Attributive verbs are placed after nouns

*aoyu hana* <br>
blue flower

Locatives:

Ringwo has two adpositions, *in* and *on*, both meaning the same in English.
Other positions is expressed by placement nouns, e.g. nouns for directions.

*schogho natta in vardu no jon* <br>
The girl is crying in the middle of the forest.

*urusaye in casa cudasai* <br>
Please be silent inside the house